\\pi }{3}))$ in the form $z=x+y\\,\\text{i},$ where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. Give the **exact** values of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-$\n{{field-2}}\n$i$" 13194139534793 true] [17592186310468 1113 #uuid "365db755-fb52-5c7a-bece-457b5b41e9cb" 13194139534793 true] [17592186310468 1145 "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nFirst, we find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the values and expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =16(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})) \\\\  & =16(\\frac{1}{2}-\\text{i}\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{2}-\\text{i}\\,\\frac{16\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =8-8\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534793 true] [17592186310469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310470 13194139534793 true] [17592186310469 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534793 true] [17592186310469 1041 "field-1" 13194139534793 true] [17592186310469 1104 #edb/ref 17592186310470 13194139534793 true] [17592186310469 1110 #uuid "fcdfea74-cd57-53fa-a391-fbee5222088f" 13194139534793 true] [17592186310470 1034 "8" 13194139534793 true] [17592186310470 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534793 true] [17592186310470 1111 #uuid "22836923-fc03-5402-8141-9222a52d1559" 13194139534793 true] [17592186310471 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310472 13194139534793 true] [17592186310471 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534793 true] [17592186310471 1041 "field-2" 13194139534793 true] [17592186310471 1104 #edb/ref 17592186310472 13194139534793 true] [17592186310471 1110 #uuid "2c2bc6e8-f55d-59d1-adbd-4b9f68ff80c2" 13194139534793 true] [17592186310472 1034 "8\\sqrt{3}" 13194139534793 true] [17592186310472 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534793 true] [17592186310472 1111 #uuid "02d0f4e4-5e64-5947-9cef-a798925d9689" 13194139534793 true] [17592186310473 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310474 13194139534793 true] [17592186310473 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310477 13194139534793 true] [17592186310473 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310479 13194139534793 true] [17592186310473 1089 "q-321424" 13194139534793 true] [17592186310473 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534793 true] [17592186310473 1100 "Express $z=\\frac{6}{1+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give the **exact** values of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ in **radians** from $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(${{field-2}}$)+i\\operatorname{sin}⁡(${{field-3}}$))$" 13194139534793 true] [17592186310473 1113 #uuid "ae148b77-e89c-5896-b914-e16652165b9b" 13194139534793 true] [17592186310473 1145 "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{6}{1+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})}{(1+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})(1-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})}{{1}^{2}-(\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})}{1-(-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(1-\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{27}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\frac{3}{2}>0$ and $y=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}<0,$ $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{|y|}{x}) \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}}{\\frac{3}{2}}) \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}))\n$$" 13194139534793 true] [17592186310474 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310475 13194139534793 true] [17592186310474 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310476 13194139534793 true] [17592186310474 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534793 true] [17592186310474 1041 "field-1" 13194139534793 true] [17592186310474 1104 #edb/ref 17592186310475 13194139534793 true] [17592186310474 1110 #uuid "a28a222d-f0d5-5bac-804e-c69e6b02c9f7" 13194139534793 true] [17592186310475 1034 "3" 13194139534793 true] [17592186310475 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534793 true] [17592186310475 1111 #uuid "a9bc2dd0-cf2a-5f41-9231-eda114df3c3e" 13194139534793 true] [17592186310476 1034 "6" 13194139534793 true] [17592186310476 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534793 true] [17592186310476 1111 #uuid "09232dfa-fe99-536e-80da-2db98dd035ff" 13194139534793 true] [17592186310477 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310478 13194139534793 true] [17592186310477 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534793 true] [17592186310477 1041 "field-2" 13194139534793 true] [17592186310477 1104 #edb/ref 17592186310478 13194139534793 true] [17592186310477 1110 #uuid "3fffe176-6bcc-59ec-b8e8-030c88ae46f6" 13194139534793 true] [1759218631